{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-tiberias", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-01-sea-called-tiberias", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus: Antipas 'built a city... called it Tiberias... in the best part of Galilee, at the lake of Gennesareth' - the city founded and named for Tiberius on this lake before the scene of 6:1; the lake's alternative name followed the city (naming documented by the Sea of Galilee article)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-422-425", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-04-passover-near", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus preserves Cestius Gallus's count of the Passover pilgrims (c. 66 CE): Passover kept as the great pilgrimage feast a generation later; it dates nothing in John 6 itself."}
{"from": "artifact:denarius", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-07-two-hundred-denarii", "grade": "C", "note": "The denarius was the standard Roman silver coin and the gospel wage-coin: two hundred denarii is the same large estimate as Mark 6:37, about eight months' wages; it attests the coin and its scale, not the purchase."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-sotah-2-1", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-09-barley-loaves", "grade": "C", "note": "In the sotah ritual 'all other meal-offerings are brought from wheat, and this one is brought from barley... her offering is animal food, i.e., barley and not wheat' (Rabban Gamliel) - barley as the inferior grain. The Mishnah was redacted c. 200 CE and may not preserve 1st-century usage exactly."}
{"from": "text:psalms-of-solomon-17", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-15-make-him-king", "grade": "C", "note": "A 1st-century BCE Jewish psalm asks God to 'raise up... their king, the son of David' - the messianic expectation that could drive a crowd to force kingship on a wonder-worker; the psalm never mentions Jesus or this event."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-17-to-capernaum", "grade": "C", "note": "A 1st-century fishing boat (8.2 m long) recovered from the lake bed: the kind of open boat that crossed to Capernaum. Context only; no link to Jesus is claimed."}
{"from": "observation:sea-of-galilee-dimensions", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-19-stadia", "grade": "C", "note": "The lake measures about 21 km long and 13 km wide, and a stadion was 150-210 m: 25-30 stadia is roughly 4-6 km, a real mid-lake distance for this crossing. It attests the distance's plausibility, never the miracle."}
{"from": "artifact:galilee-boat", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-23-boats-from-tiberias", "grade": "C", "note": "A 1st-century fishing boat recovered from the lake: the working small-craft world behind boats coming from Tiberias. Context only."}
{"from": "site:capernaum-synagogue", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-06-59-synagogue-capernaum", "grade": "D", "note": "A synagogue stood at Capernaum, but the standing white limestone building is 4th-5th century and only the basalt foundation beneath it is suggested (Loffreda) to be 1st-century - the identification of the very synagogue of 6:59 is disputed."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-07-two-hundred-denarii", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-06-37-two-hundred-denarii", "note": "John names Philip as the speaker of the two-hundred-denarii estimate; Mark leaves the disciple unnamed."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-08-andrew-told", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-17-five-loaves-two-fish", "note": "John names Andrew, Simon Peter's brother, as the one who reported the loaves; Matthew's speakers are unnamed disciples."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-09-barley-loaves", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-14-17-five-loaves-two-fish", "note": "John alone says the five loaves were barley loaves and that a boy had them; Matthew says only 'five loaves and two fish'."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-17-to-capernaum", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mk-06-45-toward-bethsaida", "note": "John names Capernaum as the boat's destination; Mark names Bethsaida (Matthew 14:22 says only 'the other side')."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-14-prophet-into-world", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:deuteronomy-18-15", "note": "Deuteronomy 18:15 promises a prophet like Moses; 'the prophet who comes into the world' echoes that expectation - a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-31-manna-cited", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:exodus-16-manna", "note": "Exodus 16:4-15: Yahweh rains bread from the sky and Moses says 'It is the bread which Yahweh has given you to eat' - the manna story the crowd cites; a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-31-manna-cited", "type": "related_to", "to": "text:psalm-78-24", "note": "John's quotation 'He gave them bread out of heaven to eat' follows the Greek wording of Psalm 78:24; the WEB has 'He rained down manna on them to eat, and gave them food from the sky.'"}
{"from": "text:isaiah-54-13", "type": "related_to", "to": "passage:jn-06-45", "note": "John 6:45 quotes 'They will all be taught by God' from Isaiah 54:13 ('All your children will be taught by Yahweh' in WEB); a cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-42-son-of-joseph", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-13-55-tekton-son", "note": "The Capernaum crowd knows Jesus as 'the son of Joseph' (6:42); the Nazarenes call him 'the carpenter's son' (Matthew 13:55) - the same family knowledge in two towns."}
{"from": "claim:jn-06-68-69-peters-confession", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:mt-16-16-peters-confession", "note": "The same acclamation ('You are the Christ, the Son of the living God') placed by John after the bread-of-life discourse in Capernaum's synagogue, not at Caesarea Philippi as in Matthew 16:16."}
